{"name":"Brake Light Trails","hex":"#ee0033","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"brake","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A device used to slow or stop the motion of a wheel, or of a vehicle, usually by friction (although other resistive forces, such as electromagnetic fields or aerodynamic drag, can also be used); also, the controls or apparatus used to engage such a mechanism such as the pedal in a car. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","The act of braking, of using a brake to slow down a machine or vehicle"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To operate a brake or brakes.","To be stopped or slowed (as if) by braking."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brake"},{"term":"light","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye (about 400–750 nanometers): visible light.","Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye or in nearby ranges (infrared or ultraviolet radiation)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To start (a fire).","To set fire to; to set burning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/light"},{"term":"trails","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of trail"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of trail"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/trails"}],"entries":[{"title":"Automotive lighting","blocks":[{"text":"Automotive lighting is functional exterior lighting in vehicles. A motor vehicle has lighting and signaling devices mounted to or integrated into its front, rear, sides, and, in some cases, top. Various devices have the dual function of illuminating the road ahead for the driver, and making the vehicle visible to others, with indications to them of turning, slowing or stopping, etc., with lights also indicating the size of some large vehicles."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Reisebus.Heck.jpg/330px-Reisebus.Heck.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Automotive_lighting","wikidata":"Q964912"},{"title":"Brake","blocks":[{"text":"A brake is a mechanical device that inhibits motion by absorbing energy from a moving system. It is used for slowing or stopping a moving vehicle, wheel, axle, or to prevent its motion, most often accomplished by means of friction."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Disc_brake.jpg/330px-Disc_brake.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brake","wikidata":"Q1534839"},{"title":"Trail","blocks":[{"text":"A trail, also known as a path or track, is a dirt road or a small paved road, usually through an undeveloped natural area, that is designed primarily for use by pedestrians, equestrians and non-motorized vehicles, although occasionally some off-roading motor vehicles may also use it. In the United Kingdom and Ireland, footpath or hiking trail is the preferred term. However, it is sometimes applied to highways in North America. In the United States, the term was historically used to describe the westward expansion trails, which are land routes into or through wildness regions of the American West used by explorers and migrants."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Trail_between_two_fields_%28Slovenia%2C_Selo_pri_Mirni%29.jpg/330px-Trail_between_two_fields_%28Slovenia%2C_Selo_pri_Mirni%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trail","wikidata":"Q628179"},{"title":"Light","blocks":[{"text":"Light, visible light, or visible radiation is electromagnetic radiation that can be perceived by the human eye. Visible light spans the visible spectrum and is usually defined as having wavelengths in the range of 400–700 nanometers (nm), corresponding to frequencies of 750–420 terahertz. The visible band sits adjacent to the infrared and the ultraviolet, called collectively optical radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light","wikidata":"Q9128"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Foglights.jpg/500px-Foglights.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Foglights.jpg/500px-Foglights.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Selective yellow fog lights","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFoglights.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Cornering_lamp_on_Oldsmobile_98.jpg/500px-Cornering_lamp_on_Oldsmobile_98.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Cornering_lamp_on_Oldsmobile_98.jpg/500px-Cornering_lamp_on_Oldsmobile_98.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A cornering light on a 1983 Oldsmobile 98","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACornering_lamp_on_Oldsmobile_98.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/2018_Dodge_Grand_Caravan_SE_in_silver_-_detail_of_light_-_front_left.jpg/500px-2018_Dodge_Grand_Caravan_SE_in_silver_-_detail_of_light_-_front_left.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/2018_Dodge_Grand_Caravan_SE_in_silver_-_detail_of_light_-_front_left.jpg/1280px-2018_Dodge_Grand_Caravan_SE_in_silver_-_detail_of_light_-_front_left.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_conten